Predicate, consumer, and supplier.

Function computes a result from an input. The other three core interfaces cover the shapes where something's missing: a boolean-shaped output, with predicate; no output at all, with consumer; or no input at all, with supplier. Together with function, these four are the vocabulary you'll reach for constantly — and recognising them by shape is most of what reading functional Java is.

Take predicate first — a test that returns a boolean. Predicate of T takes a T and answers true or false, and its method is test. So predicate of string, s, arrow, s is blank, tested on two spaces, gives true. Or predicate of Integer, n, arrow, n modulo two equals zero, for even numbers. This is the shape of a filter: stream dot filter takes a predicate, and list dot remove-if takes a predicate. Whenever you're deciding "does this element qualify?", you're writing a predicate.

Next, consumer — do something, return nothing. Consumer of T takes a T and returns void; it exists purely for its side effect, and its method is accept. So consumer of string, s, arrow, print s, when you call accept of "hi", prints and returns nothing. And list dot for-each is itself typed to take a consumer. For-each is the canonical consumer sink: hand each element to a consumer. Reach for it when you want to act on values — log them, add them somewhere — rather than compute from them.

Then, supplier — produce on demand, with no input. Supplier of T takes nothing and returns a T, and its method is get. So supplier of double, empty-parens, arrow, math dot random, gives a fresh value on each call to get. Or supplier of a list, assigned array-list double-colon new. A supplier is a deferred, or lazy, value — the work happens only when get is called, if ever. And that laziness is the point: or-else-get takes a supplier, and logger methods take suppliers, so an expensive default or message is computed only when it's actually needed.

The same four generalise. There are two-argument versions — bi-predicate and bi-consumer; map dot for-each, for instance, takes a bi-consumer of key and value. And there are primitive forms — int-predicate, int-consumer, int-supplier, boolean-supplier — to avoid boxing in numeric code. You don't memorise the list; you just recognise the pattern. A "bi" prefix means two inputs. An "int", "long", or "double" prefix means a primitive.

So the whole vocabulary reduces to one question: what goes in, and what comes out? Supplier — nothing in, a T out — for a lazy value or a factory. Consumer — a T in, nothing out — for a side effect, like for-each. Predicate — a T in, a boolean out — for a test, like filter or remove-if. And function — a T in, an R out — for a transform, like map. Learn to read a method's signature and know which shape it wants: filter wants a predicate, map wants a function, for-each wants a consumer, or-else-get wants a supplier. With these four internalised, the standard library's functional methods become self-explanatory. And when none of them fits your domain, you write your own functional interface — which is next.
